  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Odontogenic tumors are defined by the World Health Organization (WHO) as “lesions derived from epithelial, ectomesen chymal and/or mesenchymal elements that still are, or have been, part of the toothforming apparatus”.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Epulis(Greekepi-oulonmeaning “on the gum”) is a nonspecific, clinical term used to describe tumors and tumor-like masses of the gingiva.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Odontogenic tumors are categorized into three broad groups with specific tumor types in each category:",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Tumors of odontogenic epithelium:",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "conventional ameloblastoma; canine acanthomatous ameloblastoma; amyloidproducing odontogenic tumor.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Tumors of odontogenic epithelium",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "with odontogenic ectomesenchyme: ameloblastic fibroma, ameloblastic fibro-dentinoma, and ameloblastic fibro-odontoma; feline inductive odontogenic tumor; odontoma.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Tumors of mesenchyme and/or odontogenic",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "ectomesenchyme: peripheral odontogenic fibroma; feline “epulides”; odontogenic myxoma/myxofibroma. We focus here on the most common odontogenic tumors:",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Conventional ameloblastoma (CA):",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Benign but expansile, locular to multilocular",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "tumor of the jaws in dogs and cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Differs in biological behavior from the",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "more common CAA.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Canine acanthomatous ameloblastoma (CAA):",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Benign but invasive tumor of the jaws.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Typically more invasive than expansile.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "No apparent human analogue.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Amyloid-producing odontogenic tumor (APOT):",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Benign but expansile tumor of the jaws",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "affecting primarily dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Similar biological behavior to the CA",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "with a propensity to produce amyloid.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Feline inductive odontogenic tumor (FIOT):",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Benign odontogenic tumor unique to cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Generally expansile and locular with",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "epicenter at an impacted and/or malformed maxillary canine tooth.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Peripheral odontogenic fibroma (POF):",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Benign tumor primarily composed of",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "periodontal ligament-like stroma arising from tissue near the alveolar margin.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Current debate as to whether this is a",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "tumor or a reactive lesion.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Feline “epulides”:",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Clinically presents as a typical epulis.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Not clear whether this lesion is analogous",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "to POF of dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1070,
              "printed_page": 988,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
